<ArticleLayout title="How to read a supplement label" description="A practical starting point for serving sizes, ingredient lists, amounts, and the claims around them." category="Supplements" categoryHref="/categories/supplements/" date="2026-09-28" related={[guides[1], ...reviewItems.filter((item) => item.category === 'Supplements')]}>
  <div slot="contents" class="contents-list"><a href="#start-with-serving">Start with the serving</a><a href="#read-the-ingredients">Read the ingredients</a><a href="#look-at-claims">Look at the claims</a><a href="#keep-the-context">Keep the context</a></div>
  <p>A supplement label can tell you a great deal about a product, but only if you read its parts together. The front of the package usually highlights a benefit. The useful details are often in the supplement facts panel, ingredient list, directions, and warnings.</p>
  <h2 id="start-with-serving">Start with the serving</h2>
  <p>Find the serving size and the number of servings per container first. Amounts in the facts panel typically refer to that serving, not necessarily to one capsule, scoop, or bottle. Check the directions to understand how the suggested use relates to the listed amounts.</p>
  <div class="editorial-box"><strong>A useful question</strong><p>Is the headline amount for one serving, for a daily suggested use, or for the whole package?</p></div>
  <h2 id="read-the-ingredients">Read the ingredients</h2>
  <p>Look for the named ingredients and their amounts. A list of ingredients without individual quantities makes direct comparison harder. Also review the other ingredients, which can include capsule materials, sweeteners, flavors, or other components that matter to your preferences.</p>
  <p>Different products may use different forms of a similar ingredient. The name on the front of the package may not describe the complete formulation, so use the full label when comparing products.</p>
  <h2 id="look-at-claims">Look at the claims</h2>
  <p>Marketing language and label facts serve different purposes. Ask what a claim specifically promises, whether the label explains the basis for it, and whether that explanation applies to the finished product. A confident statement is not, by itself, evidence.</p>
  <h2 id="keep-the-context">Keep the context</h2>
  <p>Read directions, warnings, storage information, and manufacturer details before drawing conclusions. Consider whether the product suits your circumstances, and speak with a qualified health professional about personal health questions, especially if you take medication or manage a condition.</p>
  <p>Good comparison starts with complete information. If a label leaves important details unclear, that uncertainty belongs in the decision.</p>
</ArticleLayout>
